Motor Neurone Disease (MND)
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Progressive bulbar/limb weakness, Mixed upper/lower motor neuron signs, Preserved sensation/sphincter function

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-027
Reason for Visit: Acute distress following a severe fall caused by progressive leg weakness, resulting in an inability to get up from the floor.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a vulnerable patient with advanced neurological weakness who suffered a mechanical fall and has been stranded on the floor for two hours due to profound bilateral leg spasticity and weakness. Urgent in-person assessment is required to rule out acute fractures, assess for rhabdomyolysis or pressure injury, and provide immediate mechanical lifting assistance and pain relief.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs just gave way. I hit my hip hard, and I don't have the strength in my arms to pull myself back into the chair."
History of Present Illness: The patient has an established diagnosis of Motor Neurone Disease, primarily characterized by progressive, relentless, and distinct asymmetrical limb weakness. Over the last three months, the patient has developed progressive, severe muscle atrophy and distinct gross wasting in the upper extremities, alongside profound spasticity in the lower extremities. Today, while attempting to pivot to the toilet, the legs collapsed, leading to a direct fall onto the left hip. The patient denies hitting their head or losing consciousness. The patient has a baseline of progressive bulbar involvement resulting in a distinctly weak, hoarse voice, which is currently exacerbating their distress. The patient confirms strict, undeniable preservation of all sensory modalities; they can feel the hard floor and the pain in their hip perfectly. Sphincter function remains intact, but the patient is distressed about the inability to reach the bathroom.
Functional Status: Highly restricted mobility; reliant on a walker but frequently ignoring it due to apathy.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent, catastrophic falls; potential for long lies if the caregiver is absent.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/90 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Lying on the carpeted floor, visibly exhausted, grimacing when the left leg is moved.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal examination of the left hip reveals no external rotation or shortening of the leg. Palpation of the greater trochanter elicits moderate tenderness, but active (though weak) and passive range of motion of the hip is preserved without severe groin pain, effectively ruling out a displaced intracapsular fracture. Neurological examination reveals mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs: extreme hyperreflexia and spasticity in the lower limbs, with profound atrophy and fasciculations in the bilateral deltoids and intrinsic hand muscles. Sensation to light touch and pinprick is completely normal globally.
Environmental Observations: Loose rug near the bathroom door appears to be the mechanism of the fall.
Medication Reconciliation: Baclofen 10mg TDS for spasticity; patient missed the midday dose.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mechanical Fall secondary to Motor Neurone Disease - Status: Acute soft tissue injury (Left Hip)
Differentials:

Neck of Femur Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Ability to tolerate passive internal and external rotation of the hip without excruciating pain, and absence of limb shortening or external rotation.

Rhabdomyolysis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Short duration on the floor (2 hours) and preserved urine output without dark discoloration.

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The weakness is chronic, bilateral, and fits the exact known trajectory of the underlying MND, rather than a hyperacute, dense unilateral hemiparesis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a mechanical fall directly resulting from the progressive mixed motor neuron weakness and severe spasticity inherent to MND. Being stranded on the floor caused severe physical distress, acute pain, and immense psychological trauma. Delaying assessment until morning would have left the patient on the floor overnight, guaranteeing pressure necrosis, severe dehydration, and hypothermia. Urgent after-hours assessment was definitively required to clinically clear the patient of a major bony fracture, safely coordinate a mechanical hoist transfer from the floor back to the bed, and provide immediate analgesia for the soft tissue injury, thereby completely fulfilling MBS criteria for urgent, necessary care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Coordinated with local non-emergency patient transport/fire brigade for a safe, hoist-assisted lift back to the medical bed. Administered Paracetamol 1000mg and Ibuprofen 400mg orally STAT for hip contusion pain.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly utilizing the four-wheeled walker for every single transfer, the patient actively provides a wide base of support, instantly neutralizing the leg spasticity and preventing further falls.
Step 2: By allowing the caregiver to assist with clothing management in the bathroom, the patient conserves vital upper body strength, completely avoiding the extreme fatigue that led to the legs giving out.
Step 3: By taking the Baclofen exactly on schedule, the patient maintains a steady level of muscle relaxation, preventing the sudden, rigid spasms that disrupt balance and cause tripping.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to occupational therapy for home modification (hoist installation) and physical therapy for safe transfer training.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bedside commode to eliminate the need for long walks to the bathroom.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to assess hip pain and mobility status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department via ambulance if the hip pain becomes suddenly unbearable, if you absolutely cannot bear any weight on the left leg tomorrow, or if you develop new difficulty breathing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Clinical examination suggests a severe bruise, but a hairline fracture can occasionally be missed without an X-ray.
Clinical Course: The hip will be stiff and painful for 7-10 days. The progressive weakness from the MND will continue, making environmental modifications absolutely critical for future safety.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for review if a massive, hard swelling develops over the hip, or if the patient becomes suddenly confused or feverish.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The fall was caused by the disease making the leg muscles stiff and weak at the same time. The hip does not appear broken, just badly bruised. The painkillers will take the edge off, but the most important treatment now is using equipment to prevent the next fall, as the muscles will not regain their strength.
Actionable Steps: Use the commode next to the bed tonight. Do not attempt to walk to the bathroom. Take the muscle relaxant tablets precisely on time.

